// TWO GuardRel-correlated `since` policies in ONE policy set — the
// multi-policy compilation path. The DSQL backend compiles every temporal
// leaf of a policy set into a single SQL function, namespacing each leaf's
// CTEs and state-table rows only by a per-policy prefix (`p0_`/`p1_` in CTE
// names, `:p0_N`/`:p1_N` in `since_guard`/`since_anchor` row ids).
//
// Both leaves correlate on `context.input.user`, so their guard streaks are
// keyed by the SAME k0 hash values (md5 of the user) — the row-id prefix is
// the ONLY thing separating dave's Transfer streak (policy 0's breaker)
// from dave's Refund streak (policy 1's breaker). The guard predicates
// differ (Transfer vs Refund), so any cross-policy read flips a verdict:
//
//   @20 Approve dave — dave DID transfer (@10) → deny.
//   @30 Review dave  — dave never refunded → PERMIT; if policy 1's lookup
//                      read policy 0's rows (id mixup), it would see dave's
//                      Transfer streak and wrongly deny.
//   @50 Review eve   — eve DID refund (@40) → deny.
//   @60 Approve eve  — eve never transferred → PERMIT; the mirror detector
//                      for pollution in the other direction.
//
// Every single-policy GuardRel case compiles a one-prefix function, so this
// is the only coverage of prefix isolation for the correlation machinery.
// Interpreter is the oracle (see expected_1.out).
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Approve",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    !Drupe::Action::"Transfer"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
    since within 1h
    Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: "carol" }
};

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Review",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    !Drupe::Action::"Refund"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
    since within 1h
    Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: "carol" }
};
